  "scroll_test": "Frame one is a live circuit whose meter already reads 0.20 A beside a two-second yes-or-no question about doubling, so a scrolling 16-year-old can bet on the answer before the swipe and stays to see the meter settle it.",
  "surprise": "Twice the push gives exactly twice the flow on the meter, yet the bulb in your own room breaks the rule the moment it gets hot.",
  "outcome": "After 60 s the learner can find the current through a fixed resistor at a new voltage using V = I × R, and say it holds only at constant temperature.",
  "prerequisite_bridge": "Voltage pushes, resistance holds back, current is the flow the meter counts (SIM-push-flow).",
  "fact_sheet": [
   {
    "fact": "I is proportional to V at constant temperature",
    "node": "CLM-ohm-proportional"
   },
   {
    "fact": "V ÷ I is the resistance; V = I × R",
    "node": "CLM-resistance-constant"
   },
   {
    "fact": "10 Ω: 2 V → 0.20 A, 4 V → 0.40 A, 6 V → 0.60 A; V ÷ I = 10 Ω at all three",
    "node": "FIX-ohm-pd-02"
   },
   {
    "fact": "a hot filament's resistance rises, so its current gives less than double (qualitative flag only)",
    "node": "CLM-filament"
   }
  ],
  "causal_chain": [
   "The cell pushes; the resistor holds the flow back; the meter counts it.",
   "Change only the push; keep the resistor the same.",
   "The flow changes by the same factor.",
   "So V = I × R, while the temperature stays constant."
  ],
